WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.740
So I'm a science policy analyst
at the National Institutes

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:09.550
of Health.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.260
For those of you
that don't know,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.780
the NIH is the largest
funder of biomedical research

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.250
in the world.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.040
It consists of 27
different institutes each

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.390
each with a different
focus on human health.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.580
I work at one of those
institutes, the National

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.700
Institute of Allergy
and Infectious Diseases.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.510
You may, this guy
may look familiar.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:33.070
You may even own a bobblehead
or a pair of Dr. Fauci socks.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:38.920
But he's the director of NIAID and
has been for almost the past 40

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:39.700
years.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.490
And NIAID's mission
is to fund research

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.970
in infectious diseases,
biodefense and immune

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:50.260
mediated diseases such
as asthma and allergy.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.800
So a little bit about myself.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.340
I was a cancer researcher
for a little

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.260
for a little over a decade.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.260
I started at the University
of Arizona Cancer Center,

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.390
and then I moved to the
National Cancer Institute

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:02.800
in Bethesda, Maryland.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.019
And I tell you this
because I beg you,

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:08.020
please, no infectious
disease questions.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.720
You'll be disappointed.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.360
But while at the NCI,
I developed a love

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.550
for science policy.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.820
I then left the lab and
started a new position

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.760
at NIAID and the Data
Analytics and Research Branch.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.920
And there I was
introduced to R

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.510
and all of the
tools and packages

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.750
that I'll be
talking about today.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.320
So when I joined NIAID, I
joined a very small team

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.990
as me and two other
analysts, and I kind of

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.180
felt like this is what we do.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.820
This is how I felt the team was.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.700
We were small and
scrappy, but we really

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.600
wanted to have an impact on a
large organization like NIAID.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.910
And today, I'm going
to tell you about how

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:52.930
we use Shiny to support NIAID
and make our team a lot more

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.073
efficient as well.

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:56.740
So the first example,
I want to give you

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.700
is a Shiny dashboard to hit
research funding targets.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.400
So why do we have funding
targets for a specific research

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.200
area? First,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.720
we're a government
organization, so Congress

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.539
can tell us we'd like you to
spend this amount of money

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.890
on this research area so it can
be congressionally mandated.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.450
We also sometimes
receive targeted funding

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.540
aside from the NIH budget.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.240
One example of this recently
was Operation Warp Speed

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.340
for COVID vaccine development.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.710
And finally, NIAID
leadership can also

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.640
set funding goals based on
NIAID's mission and priorities

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.620
at the time.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.840
But when I first got
involved in this process,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.300
I found out it was a lot more
complex than I originally

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:38.530
imagined.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.510
So in some research areas
that we're tracking,

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.880
there are over 700
different projects.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.390
These are grants
and contracts that

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.570
represent a lot of
different moving parts.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.500
To give you an idea,
in some of these areas,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.920
we're tracking over half a
billion dollars worth of funding.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.350
And another added
layer of complexity

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.590
is that at NIH, we
receive grant applications

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:00.700
throughout the year.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.090
So we're stuck making
important funding decisions

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.620
at the beginning
of the year when

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.623
we don't have all of the
data available to us,

00:03:07.623 --> 00:03:09.040
so we don't have
all of the grants

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.830
that we're going
to fund that year.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.080
But I wanted to use
an analogy to help

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.607
understand this process.

00:03:15.607 --> 00:03:18.190
So you can imagine that hitting
these research funding targets

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.500
are sort of like a road trip, right?

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.000
So on a road trip,
you have to know

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.010
exactly where you are
at any given point

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.470
and how you're going to
get to your destination.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.840
Otherwise, you're
going to get lost.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.350
But we all know
it's not that easy.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.080
Sometimes things
come along that make

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.060
it difficult to get
where you want to go.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.620
I'm thinking of
one specific road

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.220
trip I took moving from
Tucson to Bethesda, Maryland.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.520
On this road trip,
I got a flat tire.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.370
My air conditioning broke in
the middle of a heat wave,

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.640
and I had a very vocal,
very disgruntled cat

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.910
in the back seat
for 2,300 miles.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.860
But today, I want to
talk about the challenges

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.770
that we faced, hitting these
research funding targets

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.460
and how we use Shiny to
address those challenges.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:04.640
So first, like I said, there's
a lot of different moving parts.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.640
There's a lot of people
involved in this process

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.133
throughout the Institute.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.550
I immediately found
out that there

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.050
were a lot of competing
estimates, right?

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.027
Everybody had their own
idea of where we were.

00:04:14.027 --> 00:04:15.610
So it's like, if
you're on a road trip

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.443
and you know you're in
Dallas, somebody else

00:04:17.443 --> 00:04:20.140
thinks you're in El Paso
and another person thinks

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.830
that you're in Utah, and nobody
can agree on where you are,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.420
where you're at the moment.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.150
So it was really frustrating.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.390
Even worse is when people
would come to me with funding

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.618
estimates and have no details.

00:04:33.618 --> 00:04:36.160
So it's like somebody saying,
we're in Missouri and I'm like,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.950
there's no chance
we're in Missouri.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.000
You took a wrong exit somewhere.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.120
How did you get there?

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.310
And they would have no idea.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.620
So my team spent a
lot of time and effort

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.440
trying to figure out how
they came to that conclusion

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.170
and comparing it to
our estimate, too.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:53.230
And finally, at the beginning
of this process, a lot of people

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:58.090
were, you know, reliant on
spreadsheets and emails.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.580
And this is a big
problem because you

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.030
can imagine that immediately,
once you send that email,

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.120
that information is out of date
because at NIH we're constantly

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.640
getting new
applications and making

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.340
new awards throughout the year.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.945
So, and also people
make mistakes, right?

00:05:15.945 --> 00:05:17.320
When you're
searching your inbox,

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.690
maybe you found that email
from last week or last month.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.670
So it was hard to get
everybody on the same page.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.670
So I created the research
area tracking tool

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:31.390
to help address these questions.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.540
But how does this dashboard get
us closer to our destination?

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.420
First, behind the scenes, I
have an R Markdown document

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.780
that's pulling in data from
multiple different databases.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:45.490
I use dbplyr, so I never have
to look at SQL coding again.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.130
And so it transforms
it, formats it,

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:51.000
and writes it to a database
for our Shiny dashboard to use.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.010
And because I published
it on RStudio Connect,

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:57.040
I can schedule it to run daily
so that the information is

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.780
always up to date.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.750
So we've immediately made this
process a lot better, right?

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.050
So we have data integration,
multiple data sources

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.080
in one spot.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.780
The information is
always up to date

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.710
and people have one source that
they can go to for information.

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:15.130
Kind of like a
Google maps, right?

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:18.010
So next, I want to
address this problem

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.790
of having multiple estimates.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:23.410
And it seemed like everybody,
people around the Institute

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:25.630
had little pieces
of the puzzle,

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.880
but we could never put those
together and get the big

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:32.510
picture, so in the dashboard, I
made all of the data available.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.140
So every single--you can see
every single project

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.840
that's included or not
included in our estimate.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.980
So this is almost like having
turn by turn directions showing

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:46.300
you exactly where we are and how
we're going to get to our goal.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.550
We've made this data
available to be downloaded

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.430
at any point for any user.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:54.860
We also wanted this to be a
platform for collaboration.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.400
So you can add and remove
projects from the estimate

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:58.390
as well.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:02.080
This is an example of that
where you can add a project

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.830
and add a reason
for that change

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:07.330
so that other users can see
why you made that change.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.070
And so that the data
is always up to date,

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:12.760
the Shiny dashboard writes
all of these user inputs

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:13.670
to a database.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:19.690
So that whenever a user logs
on, it's always up to date

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:23.440
and includes all of those
user inputs as well.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.950
So the benefit of this is that
we've increased transparency

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:28.720
and collaboration in
this whole process

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.500
and hopefully we've gotten
closer to our destination.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.240
So lastly, I wanted
the dashboard

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.680
to be able to make predictions
based on an application score.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.350
So at NIH, when an
application comes in,

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:45.950
it's basically reviewed by
an expert panel of scientists

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:47.950
and they give it a score
that helps us determine

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:49.570
whether we fund it or not.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:52.000
So this dashboard can
actually make predictions

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.490
on how much we're going
to fund even before we've

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:55.990
made those awards.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.950
And since this dashboard is
based on Shiny reactivity,

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:03.490
all of the graphs, the tables,
the high level numbers,

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:08.500
everything can be changed
based on user input.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:12.560
So we've allowed users to adjust
that scoring cutoff as well.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.650
So if you're in a
meeting saying

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.440
"I want to fund all of the
applications up to this score,"

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.960
you can actually change
that in the dashboard

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.790
and see how it affects
the estimates so

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:25.090
that you can make these
important decisions

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.560
in real time.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.810
So I hope that
this dashboard has

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.260
made this process a lot easier
and maybe a little bit more

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.490
accurate as well.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:38.820
But I think in the end, my
small team benefited the most.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.669
Right, because we've
been able to automate

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:44.630
a lot of these processes
and really free us up

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.920
to take on additional projects,

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.870
right? Before, we were
spending so much time

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.270
manually updating the
data, answering questions

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.250
from colleagues
and making changes

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:59.420
that we didn't have that
much time to do other things.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.450
So the next example
I want to give you

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.890
is how we've used
machine learning

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:06.350
to classify NIAID grants.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.970
So just a little
background on grants

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.630
At NIH, there are
really two main types.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.380
One starts at NIH.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.040
So we send out a request
for applications.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.990
And this is when the NIH
asks the scientific community

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:25.310
to apply for funding in a very
specific, well-defined area.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.410
We usually use these
to stimulate research

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:29.720
in really high priority areas.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.970
The next type of application
is the investigator-

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:33.210
initiated application.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.630
So instead of
starting at NIH, we're

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:37.940
starting with that
investigator or scientist

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:40.350
that's running a lab somewhere, right?

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.880
So these make
up by far the majority

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:48.980
of our applications, greater
than 85%, And so at NIH,

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:51.800
we really have to rely on
the scientific community,

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.820
the creativity and ingenuity
that they have to come up

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:57.750
with innovative research ideas.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.400
And those ideas can be very
broad and cross-cutting or very

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:02.610
narrow and well defined.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.730
But when we get
those applications,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.230
these investigator-
initiated ones,

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.430
we don't have much
information on it.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.210
Besides that, it falls
in the broad research

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.120
areas of NIAID's mission.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:16.780
So you can imagine that this
is very difficult, right?

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.040
When you're dealing with
thousands of applications,

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.710
how do we know how
much we've funded

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:25.040
and what projects we funded
in a specific research area?

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:26.760
So we get questions
all the time.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.270
How much have you spent
on HIV vaccine research

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:33.920
or, you know, how much have you
spent on tuberculosis research

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:34.920
and what projects?

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.450
And we need to be able to
answer those questions.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.150
So how do we do that?

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:42.230
The answer is we have a team.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.530
We have a team for that.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:47.570
So every year, NIAID gets
over 9,000 grant applications

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:50.290
and we fund about 20%
of these, give or take.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.520
We then have a team
of about 3 to 6 people

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:54.590
that go through
these awarded grants

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.600
and apply codes based on
the research being proposed.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:01.970
So they have 1,300 unique
codes, over 1,300 unique codes

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.510
that they can use to
describe that research.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:06.930
And I've included a few
examples here on the right.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:09.920
So they look, are they
researching a specific disease

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.600
like Ebola or Zika?

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.800
Is the research area, you know,
new therapeutics or diagnostics,

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.990
and so on.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:20.720
But you can imagine with
thousands of grants,

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.520
this can be challenging.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:24.210
First, it's very time intensive.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:27.020
So they have to go
through thousands

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:30.620
of these awarded grants and
read the title, the abstract,

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:34.160
the specific aims, and
describe that research.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.460
Because it's so time-consuming,
we only have the resources

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.450
to code awarded grants,
the 80% of applications

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:45.290
that are unfunded we don't
have any information on.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.490
And they have a constantly
increasing workload.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:49.400
So every year we're
getting more applications

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:50.700
and giving out more awards.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.700
And especially at the
beginning of the pandemic,

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.430
this was--we saw a huge
increase in applications

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:58.220
due to COVID research.

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:00.018
And finally, because
it takes so much time,

00:12:00.018 --> 00:12:02.310
this usually isn't done until
the very end of the year.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:03.977
So we don't have real
time data on this,

00:12:03.977 --> 00:12:06.020
which would be helpful.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:08.240
So we thought this was
a great opportunity

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.460
to use supervised
machine learning

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:11.790
for grant classification.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.000
We had a database of
thousands of grants

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.730
that had been manually
coded over the past decade

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.290
that we could use to train
machine learning models.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:23.270
So here's an example
of a grant that's

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:25.700
proposing to research a
more sensitive diagnostic

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.220
for tuberculosis.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.540
When I looked up the
codes on this grant,

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.250
you could see the
disease was tuberculosis,

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.460
the research area
was diagnostics,

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.540
and it was a clinically
focused grant.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.690
So I just want to give you
one example of how we've

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:42.050
used machine learning
to classify grants

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:43.500
based on their research area.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.050
So here it was diagnostics.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:47.900
But there are five
categories, including

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.740
therapeutic, prevention,
and vaccine.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:56.660
So we took about 7,500 grants
that have been previously

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.047
been manually coded
and use them to train

00:12:59.047 --> 00:13:01.130
three different machine
learning models, including

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.170
logistic regression,
support vector machine,

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:06.170
and random forest
to compare the three.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:11.180
We then tested these models
using about 3,200 grants that

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:12.800
had also been manually coded.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.710
And so what we found was
the logistic regression

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:19.340
was the most accurate, 88.5%,
and it also did the best

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:22.370
at predicting this relatively
small category of grants

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:25.490
in the prevention class.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.730
So we use this machine, this
logistic regression model,

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:31.890
to build a Shiny dashboard
for coding NIAID grants.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:35.540
And we then expanded
it to assist in coding

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:38.090
in five additional categories.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:40.070
So behind the
scenes, once again,

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:42.980
we have an R Markdown document
that's pulling in the data

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:47.000
from multiple sources,
making those machine learning

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.740
predictions and writing
this data to a database.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.160
It's constantly getting
updated with new applications,

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:56.690
new awards, and this is
what our Shiny dashboard uses.

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:58.740
And we once again,
we wanted people to

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.220
be able to collaborate.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:07.560
So it's writing user inputs
and a database for future use.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:10.620
So here's an example of
how we're using those.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.610
We're incorporating
user feedback.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:14.850
So you can actually go
through each project

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.310
and say whether
it's relevant or not

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:21.720
relevant to that specific
area where we're including

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:22.930
the machine learning score.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:28.230
So they know the probability
that project is in that area.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.240
And once again,
we wanted it to be

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:31.810
a platform for collaboration.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:34.470
So you can attach notes
to each of these projects

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:38.700
for other users or even
yourself in the future.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:39.880
Here's an example of that.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:42.450
You see a tiny little
envelope when a note

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.010
is attached to your project.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:45.520
We also have tags involved.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.312
So if you don't want
to write a whole note,

00:14:47.312 --> 00:14:49.350
you can just add a
small tag or phrase.

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:52.170
For example, if somebody
has verified that project,

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:54.970
you can just quickly add that.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:56.920
So we're hoping
that this project

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:59.330
will improve productivity.

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:02.732
So if previously it took
5 minutes to code a grant,

00:15:02.732 --> 00:15:04.690
we're hoping with a
machine learning prediction

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:07.840
and a probability estimate
that we can cut that time down.

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.138
And even if it was
one to 2 minutes,

00:15:10.138 --> 00:15:11.680
you could imagine
that with thousands

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:14.920
of grants, that's a very
significant time savings.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.390
We also want to support
data driven decision making.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.810
So as I mentioned, 80%
of our applications

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:22.610
are unfunded and
therefore not coded.

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.570
But with these machine
learning predictions,

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:28.360
we can apply these codes
to all grant applications

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.660
and hopefully we
can identify gaps

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:33.340
where researchers
aren't applying

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:37.660
and we can then
address those gaps.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:41.650
So I want to thank my small
team for introducing me

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:43.990
to R and all of the
tools and packages

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.420
that I've shown you today.

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:49.150
I think we've definitely been
able to have an outsized impact

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:50.660
for such a small team.

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.883
And, you know, I hope we can use
the lessons that we've learned

00:15:53.883 --> 00:15:55.300
and the tools that
we've developed

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:57.490
to continue to support
NIAID in the future

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:01.180
with their goal of, you know,
to better understand, treat

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:03.850
and ultimately prevent
infectious diseases

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:05.740
and to improve human health.

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:07.890
So thank you.

